    "text": "A resonance tube is old and has a jagged end. It is still used in the laboratory to determine the velocity of sound in air. A tuning fork of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>512</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math> produces first resonance when the tube is filled with water to a mark <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math> below a reference mark, near the open end of the tube. The experiment is repeated with another fork of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>256</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>Hz</mi></math> which produces first resonance when water reaches a mark <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>27</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math> below the reference mark. The velocity of sound in air, obtained in the experiment, is close to",
